index e8afe5a..080add8 100644
--- a/docs/configuration.md
+++ b/docs/configuration.md
@@ -841,6 +841,43 @@ kiln memory forget test-runner     # deletes every version of this key
 kiln memory restore 12             # restores this project's version as a new record
 ```
 
+An export can be carried to another project or machine and imported back:
+
+```sh
+kiln memory import memory.json                       # into the current project scope
+kiln memory global import prefs.json                 # into the global preferences scope
+kiln memory import memory.json --dry-run             # validate and report, write nothing
+kiln memory import memory.json --on-conflict replace # error (default) | skip | replace
+```
+
+`import` reads exactly one JSON array in the `export` format and does not start
+a model. It validates every record, then takes the highest-numbered version of
+each key as the record to import — the exported correction history itself is not
+replayed. Imported scope, id, creation time and lineage are never trusted:
+records are stored in the destination scope (the current project, or the global
+preferences scope with `global`), with fresh ids and creation times, while
+expiry is preserved. Input is rejected when it is malformed, not a JSON array,
+carries trailing data or unknown fields, exceeds 4 MiB or 1000 records, repeats
+or non-positively numbers an id, or breaks the field, kind or expiry limits an
+ordinary write enforces. An empty array is valid and imports nothing.
+
+File-dependent records are rebound into the current project: each source's
+original root-relative path is resolved under this project's root and its
+SHA-256 re-verified against the real file, so a record whose file is missing,
+changed, oversized, non-regular, or escapes the project (including via symlink)
+is refused — historical versions included. A `global` import accepts
+preference records without file sources only.
+
+`--on-conflict` decides what happens when a key already has an active record in
+the destination (even if expired or stale): `error` (the default) rejects the
+whole batch, `skip` leaves the existing record untouched, and `replace` stores a
+new current version while preserving the local correction history (so `restore`
+can still recover the prior value). The whole batch applies in one transaction;
+any validation or write failure, and every dry run, leaves all stored memories,
+search entries and source bindings unchanged. On success `import` prints one
+JSON object — `inserted`, `replaced`, `skipped` and `dry_run` — counting the
+affected current keys.
+
